1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,725
Real servers produce text: logs,

2
00:00:02,725 --> 00:00:04,810
lists and configuration files.

3
00:00:04,810 --> 00:00:10,441
Searching and reshaping that text is one of the most useful skills in operations.

4
00:00:10,991 --> 00:00:12,952
Today you learn six tools.

5
00:00:12,952 --> 00:00:13,329
grep,

6
00:00:13,329 --> 00:00:14,084
to search.

7
00:00:14,084 --> 00:00:15,064
Cut and sort,

8
00:00:15,064 --> 00:00:18,006
to pick columns and put lines in order.

9
00:00:18,006 --> 00:00:18,307
sed,

10
00:00:18,307 --> 00:00:19,514
to replace text.

11
00:00:19,514 --> 00:00:19,816
awk,

12
00:00:19,816 --> 00:00:20,796
to calculate.

13
00:00:20,796 --> 00:00:21,475
And find,

14
00:00:21,475 --> 00:00:22,682
to locate files.

15
00:00:23,232 --> 00:00:26,027
Every demo is a real recording on Ubuntu.

16
00:00:28,077 --> 00:00:28,472
First,

17
00:00:28,472 --> 00:00:28,801
grep.

18
00:00:28,801 --> 00:00:32,159
It prints the lines of a file that match a pattern.

19
00:00:32,159 --> 00:00:34,266
Here is a small application log.

20
00:00:34,266 --> 00:00:35,714
Type: cat app dot log.

21
00:00:36,264 --> 00:00:37,022
Six lines,

22
00:00:37,022 --> 00:00:38,842
with three levels: info,

23
00:00:38,842 --> 00:00:39,979
warn and error.

24
00:00:39,979 --> 00:00:41,191
Find the errors.

25
00:00:41,191 --> 00:00:42,025
Type: grep,

26
00:00:42,025 --> 00:00:42,480
ERROR,

27
00:00:42,480 --> 00:00:43,390
app dot log.

28
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:45,809
Only the two error lines.

29
00:00:45,809 --> 00:00:48,276
Now count the info lines instead,

30
00:00:48,276 --> 00:00:49,173
with dash c.

31
00:00:49,173 --> 00:00:49,996
Type: grep,

32
00:00:49,996 --> 00:00:50,519
dash c,

33
00:00:50,519 --> 00:00:50,893
INFO,

34
00:00:50,893 --> 00:00:51,790
app dot log.

35
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:53,616
Three info lines.

36
00:00:53,616 --> 00:00:55,191
Now invert the match,

37
00:00:55,191 --> 00:00:56,091
with dash v,

38
00:00:56,091 --> 00:00:58,717
to see every line that is not info.

39
00:00:58,717 --> 00:00:59,542
Type: grep,

40
00:00:59,542 --> 00:01:00,068
dash v,

41
00:01:00,068 --> 00:01:00,443
INFO,

42
00:01:00,443 --> 00:01:01,343
app dot log.

43
00:01:01,893 --> 00:01:04,773
Those are the lines that need attention.

44
00:01:04,773 --> 00:01:05,133
Next,

45
00:01:05,133 --> 00:01:06,357
add line numbers,

46
00:01:06,357 --> 00:01:07,221
with dash n.

47
00:01:07,221 --> 00:01:08,013
Type: grep,

48
00:01:08,013 --> 00:01:08,517
dash n,

49
00:01:08,517 --> 00:01:09,093
timeout,

50
00:01:09,093 --> 00:01:09,957
app dot log.

51
00:01:10,507 --> 00:01:11,315
Line five.

52
00:01:11,315 --> 00:01:11,720
Last,

53
00:01:11,720 --> 00:01:13,175
extended patterns,

54
00:01:13,175 --> 00:01:14,791
with dash capital E.

55
00:01:14,791 --> 00:01:16,004
A bar means or.

56
00:01:16,004 --> 00:01:16,893
Type: grep,

57
00:01:16,893 --> 00:01:18,105
dash capital E,

58
00:01:18,105 --> 00:01:19,156
double quote,

59
00:01:19,156 --> 00:01:19,560
WARN,

60
00:01:19,560 --> 00:01:19,884
bar,

61
00:01:19,884 --> 00:01:20,369
ERROR,

62
00:01:20,369 --> 00:01:21,420
double quote,

63
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:22,390
app dot log.

64
00:01:22,940 --> 00:01:28,550
Warnings and errors together: three lines. One tool, four flags, and you can read any log.

65
00:01:30,600 --> 00:01:30,973
Next,

66
00:01:30,973 --> 00:01:31,942
cut and sort.

67
00:01:31,942 --> 00:01:34,329
Here is a small table of people,

68
00:01:34,329 --> 00:01:37,088
with the columns separated by commas.

69
00:01:37,088 --> 00:01:39,027
Type: cat users dot C S V.

70
00:01:39,577 --> 00:01:41,153
Three columns: name,

71
00:01:41,153 --> 00:01:42,177
team and age.

72
00:01:42,177 --> 00:01:44,857
The cut command pulls out columns.

73
00:01:44,857 --> 00:01:46,906
Dash d sets the separator,

74
00:01:46,906 --> 00:01:49,034
and dash f picks the field.

75
00:01:49,034 --> 00:01:49,822
Type: cut,

76
00:01:49,822 --> 00:01:50,373
dash d,

77
00:01:50,373 --> 00:01:50,846
comma,

78
00:01:50,846 --> 00:01:51,398
dash f,

79
00:01:51,398 --> 00:01:51,713
one,

80
00:01:51,713 --> 00:01:52,974
users dot C S V.

81
00:01:53,524 --> 00:01:54,677
Just the names.

82
00:01:54,677 --> 00:01:57,138
Now sort the whole table by age.

83
00:01:57,138 --> 00:01:59,137
Dash t sets the separator,

84
00:01:59,137 --> 00:02:01,905
dash k three picks the third column,

85
00:02:01,905 --> 00:02:04,057
and dash n sorts as numbers.

86
00:02:04,057 --> 00:02:04,903
Type: sort,

87
00:02:04,903 --> 00:02:05,441
dash t,

88
00:02:05,441 --> 00:02:05,903
comma,

89
00:02:05,903 --> 00:02:06,441
dash k,

90
00:02:06,441 --> 00:02:06,902
three,

91
00:02:06,902 --> 00:02:07,440
dash n,

92
00:02:07,440 --> 00:02:08,671
users dot C S V.

93
00:02:09,221 --> 00:02:10,302
Youngest first,

94
00:02:10,302 --> 00:02:12,392
and the header stayed on top,

95
00:02:12,392 --> 00:02:14,411
because text counts as zero.

96
00:02:14,411 --> 00:02:14,771
Next,

97
00:02:14,771 --> 00:02:16,357
count people per team.

98
00:02:16,357 --> 00:02:17,799
Cut the team column,

99
00:02:17,799 --> 00:02:18,376
sort it,

100
00:02:18,376 --> 00:02:21,403
then count the repeats with unique dash c.

101
00:02:21,403 --> 00:02:22,124
Type: cut,

102
00:02:22,124 --> 00:02:22,629
dash d,

103
00:02:22,629 --> 00:02:23,061
comma,

104
00:02:23,061 --> 00:02:23,566
dash f,

105
00:02:23,566 --> 00:02:23,854
two,

106
00:02:23,854 --> 00:02:25,008
users dot C S V,

107
00:02:25,008 --> 00:02:25,368
pipe,

108
00:02:25,368 --> 00:02:25,728
sort,

109
00:02:25,728 --> 00:02:26,089
pipe,

110
00:02:26,089 --> 00:02:27,098
unique dash c.

111
00:02:27,648 --> 00:02:28,358
Two in dev,

112
00:02:28,358 --> 00:02:29,197
three in ops,

113
00:02:29,197 --> 00:02:31,004
and the header counted once.

114
00:02:31,004 --> 00:02:33,649
Unique only collapses neighbouring lines,

115
00:02:33,649 --> 00:02:35,456
which is why you sort first.

116
00:02:37,506 --> 00:02:38,074
Now sed,

117
00:02:38,074 --> 00:02:39,351
the stream editor.

118
00:02:39,351 --> 00:02:42,330
Its most common job is search and replace.

119
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:44,813
Here is a small configuration file.

120
00:02:44,813 --> 00:02:46,658
Type: cat config dot text.

121
00:02:47,208 --> 00:02:48,337
Three settings.

122
00:02:48,337 --> 00:02:50,822
Change the mode from dev to prod.

123
00:02:50,822 --> 00:02:53,382
The s command means substitute: s,

124
00:02:53,382 --> 00:02:53,833
slash,

125
00:02:53,833 --> 00:02:54,134
old,

126
00:02:54,134 --> 00:02:54,586
slash,

127
00:02:54,586 --> 00:02:54,887
new,

128
00:02:54,887 --> 00:02:55,339
slash.

129
00:02:55,339 --> 00:02:56,092
Type: sed,

130
00:02:56,092 --> 00:02:57,071
single quote,

131
00:02:57,071 --> 00:02:59,254
s slash dev slash prod slash,

132
00:02:59,254 --> 00:03:00,233
single quote,

133
00:03:00,233 --> 00:03:01,437
config dot text.

134
00:03:01,987 --> 00:03:07,491
The mode shows prod. But did the file change? Check it. Type: cat config dot text.

135
00:03:08,041 --> 00:03:08,766
Still dev.

136
00:03:08,766 --> 00:03:11,667
The sed command only printed the change,

137
00:03:11,667 --> 00:03:13,553
and the file is untouched.

138
00:03:13,553 --> 00:03:15,293
To edit the file itself,

139
00:03:15,293 --> 00:03:16,091
use dash i.

140
00:03:16,091 --> 00:03:17,251
Change the port.

141
00:03:17,251 --> 00:03:17,976
Type: sed,

142
00:03:17,976 --> 00:03:18,484
dash i,

143
00:03:18,484 --> 00:03:19,426
single quote,

144
00:03:19,426 --> 00:03:23,922
s slash eight zero eight zero slash nine zero nine zero slash,

145
00:03:23,922 --> 00:03:24,865
single quote,

146
00:03:24,865 --> 00:03:26,025
config dot text.

147
00:03:26,575 --> 00:03:31,354
Silent, and the file has changed. Check it. Type: cat config dot text.

148
00:03:31,904 --> 00:03:34,494
The port is now nine zero nine zero.

149
00:03:34,494 --> 00:03:36,724
Dash i changes a file for good,

150
00:03:36,724 --> 00:03:38,378
so keep a backup first.

151
00:03:38,378 --> 00:03:41,111
One more trick: delete matching lines.

152
00:03:41,111 --> 00:03:41,831
Type: sed,

153
00:03:41,831 --> 00:03:42,766
single quote,

154
00:03:42,766 --> 00:03:44,133
slash mode slash d,

155
00:03:44,133 --> 00:03:45,068
single quote,

156
00:03:45,068 --> 00:03:46,219
config dot text.

157
00:03:46,769 --> 00:03:49,273
The mode line is gone from the output.

158
00:03:49,273 --> 00:03:49,668
Again,

159
00:03:49,668 --> 00:03:51,579
the file itself is unchanged,

160
00:03:51,579 --> 00:03:53,425
because there was no dash i.

161
00:03:55,475 --> 00:03:56,077
Now awk,

162
00:03:56,077 --> 00:03:58,859
a small language for columns of text.

163
00:03:58,859 --> 00:03:59,310
First,

164
00:03:59,310 --> 00:04:01,115
look at the table again.

165
00:04:01,115 --> 00:04:03,070
Type: cat users dot C S V.

166
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:06,862
Awk reads each line and splits it into fields.

167
00:04:06,862 --> 00:04:08,976
Dollar one is the first field,

168
00:04:08,976 --> 00:04:11,655
and dash capital F sets the separator.

169
00:04:11,655 --> 00:04:12,360
Type: awk,

170
00:04:12,360 --> 00:04:13,417
dash capital F,

171
00:04:13,417 --> 00:04:13,840
comma,

172
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:14,756
single quote,

173
00:04:14,756 --> 00:04:15,532
open brace,

174
00:04:15,532 --> 00:04:16,730
print dollar one,

175
00:04:16,730 --> 00:04:17,576
close brace,

176
00:04:17,576 --> 00:04:18,492
single quote,

177
00:04:18,492 --> 00:04:19,620
users dot C S V.

178
00:04:20,170 --> 00:04:21,170
The names again,

179
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:21,733
like cut.

180
00:04:21,733 --> 00:04:23,796
But awk can also test conditions.

181
00:04:23,796 --> 00:04:25,984
Print the people older than thirty.

182
00:04:25,984 --> 00:04:27,422
N R is the line number,

183
00:04:27,422 --> 00:04:30,048
and N R greater than one skips the header.

184
00:04:30,048 --> 00:04:30,673
Type: awk,

185
00:04:30,673 --> 00:04:31,611
dash capital F,

186
00:04:31,611 --> 00:04:31,986
comma,

187
00:04:31,986 --> 00:04:32,799
single quote,

188
00:04:32,799 --> 00:04:34,112
N R greater than one,

189
00:04:34,112 --> 00:04:35,175
double ampersand,

190
00:04:35,175 --> 00:04:37,238
dollar three greater than thirty,

191
00:04:37,238 --> 00:04:37,926
open brace,

192
00:04:37,926 --> 00:04:38,989
print dollar one,

193
00:04:38,989 --> 00:04:39,364
comma,

194
00:04:39,364 --> 00:04:40,177
dollar three,

195
00:04:40,177 --> 00:04:40,927
close brace,

196
00:04:40,927 --> 00:04:41,740
single quote,

197
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:42,740
users dot C S V.

198
00:04:43,290 --> 00:04:44,152
Three people.

199
00:04:44,152 --> 00:04:44,484
Last,

200
00:04:44,484 --> 00:04:45,412
a calculation.

201
00:04:45,412 --> 00:04:47,070
Add every age to a total,

202
00:04:47,070 --> 00:04:48,662
and after the last line,

203
00:04:48,662 --> 00:04:49,855
print the average.

204
00:04:49,855 --> 00:04:50,519
Type: awk,

205
00:04:50,519 --> 00:04:51,513
dash capital F,

206
00:04:51,513 --> 00:04:51,911
comma,

207
00:04:51,911 --> 00:04:52,773
single quote,

208
00:04:52,773 --> 00:04:54,166
N R greater than one,

209
00:04:54,166 --> 00:04:54,895
open brace,

210
00:04:54,895 --> 00:04:56,819
sum plus equals dollar three,

211
00:04:56,819 --> 00:04:57,614
close brace,

212
00:04:57,614 --> 00:04:57,880
END,

213
00:04:57,880 --> 00:04:58,609
open brace,

214
00:04:58,609 --> 00:04:59,670
print sum slash,

215
00:04:59,670 --> 00:05:00,930
open round bracket,

216
00:05:00,930 --> 00:05:01,858
N R minus one,

217
00:05:01,858 --> 00:05:03,185
close round bracket,

218
00:05:03,185 --> 00:05:03,981
close brace,

219
00:05:03,981 --> 00:05:04,843
single quote,

220
00:05:04,843 --> 00:05:05,904
users dot C S V.

221
00:05:06,454 --> 00:05:11,424
The average age is thirty-three point six. Awk did the maths, with no spreadsheet.

222
00:05:13,474 --> 00:05:14,693
Last tool: find.

223
00:05:14,693 --> 00:05:16,978
It searches folders for files.

224
00:05:16,978 --> 00:05:20,253
Here is a small folder with two subfolders.

225
00:05:20,253 --> 00:05:21,014
Type: L S,

226
00:05:21,014 --> 00:05:22,157
dash capital R.

227
00:05:22,707 --> 00:05:24,112
Logs in two places.

228
00:05:24,112 --> 00:05:26,406
Find every log file below here.

229
00:05:26,406 --> 00:05:29,513
The dot means start in the current folder.

230
00:05:29,513 --> 00:05:30,327
Type: find,

231
00:05:30,327 --> 00:05:30,623
dot,

232
00:05:30,623 --> 00:05:31,363
dash name,

233
00:05:31,363 --> 00:05:32,324
double quote,

234
00:05:32,324 --> 00:05:33,286
star dot log,

235
00:05:33,286 --> 00:05:34,248
double quote.

236
00:05:34,798 --> 00:05:35,933
Three log files.

237
00:05:35,933 --> 00:05:41,112
The quotes matter: they stop the shell from expanding the star too early.

238
00:05:41,112 --> 00:05:42,460
Now find old files.

239
00:05:42,460 --> 00:05:46,930
Dash M time plus seven means modified more than seven days ago.

240
00:05:46,930 --> 00:05:47,710
Type: find,

241
00:05:47,710 --> 00:05:47,994
dot,

242
00:05:47,994 --> 00:05:48,703
dash name,

243
00:05:48,703 --> 00:05:49,626
double quote,

244
00:05:49,626 --> 00:05:50,548
star dot log,

245
00:05:50,548 --> 00:05:51,470
double quote,

246
00:05:51,470 --> 00:05:52,322
dash M time,

247
00:05:52,322 --> 00:05:53,102
plus seven.

248
00:05:53,652 --> 00:05:54,964
Only the old log.

249
00:05:54,964 --> 00:05:55,350
Last,

250
00:05:55,350 --> 00:05:57,587
run a command on every match,

251
00:05:57,587 --> 00:05:58,745
with dash exec.

252
00:05:58,745 --> 00:06:02,063
The curly braces stand for each file found.

253
00:06:02,063 --> 00:06:02,912
Type: find,

254
00:06:02,912 --> 00:06:03,220
dot,

255
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:03,992
dash name,

256
00:06:03,992 --> 00:06:04,995
double quote,

257
00:06:04,995 --> 00:06:05,998
star dot log,

258
00:06:05,998 --> 00:06:07,002
double quote,

259
00:06:07,002 --> 00:06:07,773
dash exec,

260
00:06:07,773 --> 00:06:08,082
W C,

261
00:06:08,082 --> 00:06:08,622
dash l,

262
00:06:08,622 --> 00:06:09,471
open brace,

263
00:06:09,471 --> 00:06:10,397
close brace,

264
00:06:10,397 --> 00:06:10,783
plus.

265
00:06:11,333 --> 00:06:13,146
A line count for every log,

266
00:06:13,146 --> 00:06:13,952
and a total.

267
00:06:13,952 --> 00:06:18,117
Find with dash exec can run any command on thousands of files.

268
00:06:20,167 --> 00:06:26,290
Now combine the tools in a small log report. Here is the log again. Type: cat app dot log.

269
00:06:26,840 --> 00:06:28,532
Which levels appear most?

270
00:06:28,532 --> 00:06:30,564
Pull the third field with awk.

271
00:06:30,564 --> 00:06:31,579
Sort the words,

272
00:06:31,579 --> 00:06:34,355
and count the repeats with unique dash c.

273
00:06:34,355 --> 00:06:35,033
Type: awk,

274
00:06:35,033 --> 00:06:35,913
single quote,

275
00:06:35,913 --> 00:06:36,658
open brace,

276
00:06:36,658 --> 00:06:37,944
print dollar three,

277
00:06:37,944 --> 00:06:38,757
close brace,

278
00:06:38,757 --> 00:06:39,637
single quote,

279
00:06:39,637 --> 00:06:40,449
app dot log,

280
00:06:40,449 --> 00:06:40,788
pipe,

281
00:06:40,788 --> 00:06:41,127
sort,

282
00:06:41,127 --> 00:06:41,465
pipe,

283
00:06:41,465 --> 00:06:42,413
unique dash c.

284
00:06:42,963 --> 00:06:44,145
Three info lines,

285
00:06:44,145 --> 00:06:46,022
two errors and one warning.

286
00:06:46,022 --> 00:06:48,803
Now the details of each error: the time,

287
00:06:48,803 --> 00:06:49,777
and the cause.

288
00:06:49,777 --> 00:06:50,541
Type: grep,

289
00:06:50,541 --> 00:06:50,959
ERROR,

290
00:06:50,959 --> 00:06:51,793
app dot log,

291
00:06:51,793 --> 00:06:52,141
pipe,

292
00:06:52,141 --> 00:06:52,419
awk,

293
00:06:52,419 --> 00:06:53,323
single quote,

294
00:06:53,323 --> 00:06:54,087
open brace,

295
00:06:54,087 --> 00:06:55,269
print dollar two,

296
00:06:55,269 --> 00:06:55,686
comma,

297
00:06:55,686 --> 00:06:56,521
dollar four,

298
00:06:56,521 --> 00:06:57,355
close brace,

299
00:06:57,355 --> 00:06:58,259
single quote.

300
00:06:58,809 --> 00:07:03,822
A report with one line per error. Small tools, joined by pipes, do real work.

301
00:07:04,372 --> 00:07:06,762
Search. Cut. Replace. Calculate. Find.

302
00:07:08,812 --> 00:07:10,135
Four myths to clear up.

303
00:07:10,685 --> 00:07:12,869
Myth one: sed changes the file.

304
00:07:12,869 --> 00:07:13,081
No.

305
00:07:13,081 --> 00:07:14,561
It prints the change.

306
00:07:14,561 --> 00:07:16,957
Only dash i edits the file itself.

307
00:07:17,507 --> 00:07:19,770
Myth two: grep only searches files.

308
00:07:19,770 --> 00:07:19,963
No.

309
00:07:19,963 --> 00:07:22,873
It also filters the output of other commands,

310
00:07:22,873 --> 00:07:23,843
through a pipe.

311
00:07:24,393 --> 00:07:27,286
Myth three: unique removes all duplicates.

312
00:07:27,286 --> 00:07:27,493
No.

313
00:07:27,493 --> 00:07:30,042
It collapses neighbouring lines only,

314
00:07:30,042 --> 00:07:31,006
so sort first.

315
00:07:31,556 --> 00:07:37,167
Myth four: find needs a file name. No. It can also filter by type, size and age.

316
00:07:37,717 --> 00:07:40,106
Search. Cut. Replace. Calculate. Find.

317
00:07:42,156 --> 00:07:46,721
Here is how you check your own understanding. Six points, and you need all six.

318
00:07:47,271 --> 00:07:51,090
Search a file with grep, using dash c, dash v and dash n.

319
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:54,797
Cut a column, and sort lines as text and as numbers.

320
00:07:55,347 --> 00:07:58,995
Replace text with sed, and know when dash i changes the file.

321
00:07:59,545 --> 00:08:02,831
Print fields and calculate a total or an average with awk.

322
00:08:03,381 --> 00:08:06,901
Find files by name and by age, and run a command on them.

323
00:08:07,451 --> 00:08:10,096
Join the tools with pipes to build a small report.

324
00:08:10,646 --> 00:08:12,267
To pass, you need all six.

325
00:08:14,317 --> 00:08:19,117
Your turn, in your own Ubuntu virtual machine. Type every command yourself.

326
00:08:19,667 --> 00:08:24,232
One: write a small log file, and use grep to count, invert and number lines.

327
00:08:24,782 --> 00:08:29,348
Two: write a comma separated table, then cut a column and sort it by a number.

328
00:08:29,898 --> 00:08:35,295
Three: use sed to replace a word. Then use dash i, but only on a copy of the file.

329
00:08:35,845 --> 00:08:39,258
Four: use awk to add up one column, and print the average.

330
00:08:39,808 --> 00:08:44,310
Five: find files by name, then by age, and run word count on them.

331
00:08:44,860 --> 00:08:49,382
Six: build a pipeline that counts how many times each level appears in your log.

332
00:08:49,932 --> 00:08:53,239
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

333
00:09:08,789 --> 00:09:09,664
Quick check.

334
00:09:10,214 --> 00:09:12,070
Which grep flag inverts the match?

335
00:09:12,620 --> 00:09:14,241
Why do you sort before unique?

336
00:09:14,791 --> 00:09:16,818
How do you make sed edit the file itself?

337
00:09:17,368 --> 00:09:18,968
What does N R hold in awk?

338
00:09:19,518 --> 00:09:25,171
Objective covered: MC05-O04.

339
00:09:25,721 --> 00:09:27,790
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

340
00:09:38,340 --> 00:09:43,226
Next lesson: pipes, file descriptors, redirection and safe temporary files.

341
00:09:43,776 --> 00:09:47,104
